          "path": "packages/contracts/src/Raffle.sol",
          "start": 416,
          "end": 430
        },
        {
          "path": "packages/contracts/src/Raffle.sol",
          "start": 329,
          "end": 337
        }
      ],
      "evidence": [
        "acb56b908f72af85a90165d3c15c87c9ea6960e10abf18af3a0ef6b66d597831"
      ]
    },
    {
      "schemaVersion": 1,
      "id": "deaf2fe4-9bea-47cb-876e-53e4eba6c4c0",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-21T08:10:55.989Z",
      "runId": "a9a573ab-b66c-4d87-8ca0-1e7a5d5bcf78",
      "producer": "model",
      "text": "Destination screening asks only this raffle's immutable factory whether an address is one of its registered clones. A clone deployed by another RaffleFactory is therefore accepted as a sponsor recipient (and can likewise be configured as a factory treasury). In a cash/refund branch, releaseSponsorPrize uses unsafe transferFrom and moves the prize into that other clone, while quote-token releases also pass the factory-local screen and become unaccounted balances in the recipient clone. The recipient clone has no rescue path. This is a concrete cross-clone asset path contrary to the stated never-move-assets-between-clones requirement, although the party selecting the destination bears the direct loss.\nAssumptions: More than one deployment of RaffleFactory can exist and the clone-isolation requirement applies across those deployments.; A sponsor or factory deployer selects an existing clone from another factory as sponsorRecipient or protocolTreasury.; The prize token otherwise follows ordinary ERC-721 transferFrom ownership semantics.",
      "source": [
